 walk through  they scan absolutely everything they you  know watch the first three seconds of  every video  um and um  and other people um you know it really  had a  transformative experience actually with  it and you know talked about  um you know seeing artists who looked  like them understanding for the first  time  um uh you know that a  contemporary art that might be ugly to  them  um understanding the the motivations of  an artist and where it comes from really  really deep in their connection to it in  a way that  um sometimes they hadn't had before with  this whole  genre of art um  you know this product is used um in real  life and and  i would argue that that's true of of  kind of everything on some level of  everything that um  that was talked about today that um that  there's a physical context in which  people  are you know reading books  or doing data entry or  learning about art or engaging with art  and  that sort of physical disposition was  a big influence on the design of the  interface as well as on solving some of  the kinds of problems that  um that i've just alluded to  so you know early on when we were doing  testing it was important to also sort of  include the labels  and you know think about the context  that this is happening in  this is not a you know a sort of  disembodied  codex of jpegs um  uh for  the sort of fallback so that a user who  tech the image recognition does not work  very well  um on on label text it's not distinct  enough and  so but it was really important that  there be a fallback for someone who  would scan the label we learned that  from from watching visitors and also  because some of these works which i'll  talk about in a minute are  very hard to recognize um and so  in those cases well in every case  basically  there's an image a frame of the video  that you get for example which is more  reliably  which is reliably recognizable and which  also accuse the user  that accused the visitor that this work  is  in the system and one of the nice things  about having such a low barrier to entry  that is not a native application is that  there is this short url that you can  just enter into your web browser at any  time so you can start sort of anywhere  in the system and follow any any pathway  but  basically every work in the museum is in  the system with  i think with a few exceptions at this  point  and finally letting visitors know that  this is here  and that there is this additional  available layer to the experience is  actually sort of one of the biggest  challenges and um  the it's it's even more heightened  actually at the um  at the smithsonian museums on the mall  where um  there's no there's no um there's no pay  gate there's no ticketing so you really  just walk right into the museum so  there's no point at which there's no  inherent point at which  a visitor services staff can say here's  this thing that you can do  so on the one hand there's this low  barrier to entry on the other hand we  really need people to know that they can  take that step  [Music]  the exhibition that we launched with  ironically  was called what absence is made of  and you know an image recognition app  is um about the pixels it's a kind of a  ghost in the shell  um it is about dematerialized objects  so what absence is made of um was a very  poetic exhibition to start with  it featured not exclusively minimalist  and conceptual art but a lot of  a lot of minimalist and conceptual art  just  reading from the description of the  exhibition a curator described it as  the spaces left behind when something  disappears  and as work on a massive scale yet with  the barest materials  and so this is like this is um didier  vermeeran's sculpture and it's kind of  meant  in part to look like the pedestal for  other artwork  um that's fred sandbeck  it's his string that's  raynor novos black squares and these are  joseph boys is this black squares and  it turns out that once you seed even one  black square into your image recognition  database  every dark corner of the museum is now  pops up joseph boyz  and um uh so  uh peter kind of talked about unexpected  i really like seeing that fred wilson um  um uh image and um  talking about unexpected juxtapositions  we really  created a situation in which um uh  the work was sort of work that was meant  to be  curated together and tell a really  amazing story in fact matched up with  each other through this kind of  technological  prism in a way that was  a challenge as well as an opportunity  that's giovanni and selmo this hand is  not part of the artwork the artwork  is um uh is just a single slide and a  slide projector with the word visible  projecting into empty space  and um so you can only see it when you  interrupt the projection  it's also a single slide so it just  slowly burns actually and  under the in the light as it changes it  gets more and more brown every day  so this is a an artwork that is meant to  defy machine vision  um but um uh  you know we can work around those  problems often by um  by seeding the scene and by you know as  a couple of people have talked about by  making sure that we're including humans  in this process of thinking about  um sort of where the artwork  is located literally in this case um and  you know taking a couple steps back  or moving around the sculpture to seed  images that are going to be effective  and that means again watching visitors  and tests doing lots of testing  and you know i think that's a kind of um  kind of like stephen was talking about a  kind of  human knowledge that is always going to  be an essential part of these projects  especially  and will be most effective when you work  um in an interdisciplinary way  so the last image i wanted to show is  sort of the solution to the ensemble  problem and  and you know critic's going to talk  about in a work like that is the artwork  the  projection is it the situation is it the  slide projector  in our case it's the slide projector  that scans actually really effectively  and um uh so here's uh  here's that work kind of shining back  out and this is a team of  of testers from the hirshhorn and the  broader smithsonian  kind of trying to figure this stuff out  um so  we'll stop there thanks a lot  and i just wanted to add that um kind of  going into the next section of the talk  is that um  while the hershey and i was driven with  a very specific content strategy at the  hershon we believe that what we created  is applicable widely across different  subject matter so what we've done at the  smithsonian  is that we've started taking the hershel  knight and adapting it for use  across our different museums and  we're also working with third-party  external organizations and you know  trying to adapt to hershel knight for  their use as well  eventually what we hope to do is to  create a open source version of the  hershel knight  and to make it readily available for  smaller museums and organizations to  pick it up and deploy at their space  as well thank you  [Music]  you
 

